WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.106
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.406 --> 00:00:08.839
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:16.180
Unit sixteen: Public Diplomacy. Today you will introduce technical terms with 'sometimes referred to as' / 'known as'.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.330
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:23.267
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:25.408
Public diplomacy.

7
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:31.307
Public diplomacy means a government's efforts to communicate directly with foreign publics.

8
00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:35.994
Public diplomacy includes scholarships and cultural festivals.

9
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:39.758
Your turn. Say: Public diplomacy.

10
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.973
Public diplomacy.

11
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:45.811
Soft power.

12
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:51.045
Soft power means influence based on attraction rather than coercion.

13
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.416
Japan's soft power owes much to anime and cuisine.

14
00:00:55.017 --> 00:00:57.924
Your turn. Say: Soft power.

15
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:01.919
Soft power.

16
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:04.079
Cultural exchange.

17
00:01:05.083 --> 00:01:09.521
Cultural exchange means programmes in which people share cultures across borders.

18
00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:13.196
Cultural exchange brought Khmer dancers to Paris.

19
00:01:13.783 --> 00:01:16.935
Your turn. Say: Cultural exchange.

20
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.219
Cultural exchange.

21
00:01:21.983 --> 00:01:23.228
Nation branding.

22
00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:27.679
Nation branding means shaping a country's international image.

23
00:01:28.283 --> 00:01:31.641
Nation branding promotes Cambodia as 'Kingdom of Wonder'.

24
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.018
Your turn. Say: Nation branding.

25
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:39.274
Nation branding.

26
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:41.668
International relations.

27
00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:46.993
International relations means relations between states, and the study of them.

28
00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:50.477
She studied international relations in Tokyo.

29
00:01:51.083 --> 00:01:54.386
Your turn. Say: International relations.

30
00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:59.303
International relations.

31
00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:00.947
Goodwill.

32
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.482
Goodwill means friendly feelings and support between peoples.

33
00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:08.896
Disaster aid generated lasting goodwill.

34
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:11.602
Your turn. Say: Goodwill.

35
00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:15.294
Goodwill.

36
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.167
Perception.

37
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:20.984
Perception means the way a country is seen by others.

38
00:02:21.583 --> 00:02:24.755
Perception of the country improved after the festival.

39
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.200
Your turn. Say: Perception.

40
00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:32.162
Perception.

41
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:33.906
Outreach.

42
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:38.623
Outreach means efforts to connect with and inform a wider public.

43
00:02:39.217 --> 00:02:42.331
Embassy outreach included free language classes.

44
00:02:42.917 --> 00:02:45.685
Your turn. Say: Outreach.

45
00:02:48.533 --> 00:02:49.616
Outreach.

46
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.202
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.050 --> 00:03:01.754
Visal, why does Korea fund free Korean classes and K-pop events in Phnom Penh? It earns no direct profit.

48
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:13.490
It's a long-term investment in influence. This approach is sometimes referred to as soft power: winning hearts rather than forcing compliance.

49
00:03:14.583 --> 00:03:16.351
Does it really change anything?

50
00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:29.657
Considerably. Students who love Korean culture often choose to study or work there, and that goodwill can shape future political and trade relations.

51
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.575
Could Cambodia do the same?

52
00:03:33.683 --> 00:03:43.685
It already does, in a way. Programmes known as cultural diplomacy, such as touring Apsara troupes, project an image far richer than headlines alone.

53
00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:46.461
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:47.317 --> 00:03:50.802
Grammar focus: Sometimes referred to as and known as.

55
00:03:51.102 --> 00:03:52.173
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.883 --> 00:03:55.974
This approach is sometimes referred to as soft power.

57
00:03:56.883 --> 00:04:00.427
Programmes known as cultural diplomacy project a richer image.

58
00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:04.013
This strategy is often termed nation branding.

59
00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:15.934
Remember both prepositions: 'referred TO AS' (not 'referred as'). 'Known as' and 'termed' need no extra preposition. These phrases are ideal for introducing terminology in academic writing.

60
00:04:16.683 --> 00:04:20.799
Be careful! Don't say: It is sometimes referred soft power.

61
00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:25.662
Say: It is sometimes referred to as soft power.

62
00:04:25.962 --> 00:04:27.474
Referred to as.

63
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:31.360
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:33.481
Winning Hearts Abroad.

65
00:04:33.781 --> 00:04:41.679
Public diplomacy refers to a government's efforts to engage foreign citizens directly, rather than communicating only with other governments.

66
00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:49.275
Joseph Nye argued that attraction can achieve outcomes that coercion cannot, a capacity he famously termed 'soft power'.

67
00:04:50.267 --> 00:04:57.630
Scholarships, broadcasting, cultural exchange and sport are all instruments of what is sometimes referred to as 'the battle for hearts and minds'.

68
00:04:58.633 --> 00:05:04.905
Cambodia's heritage, particularly Angkor, gives the country considerable potential for nation branding.

69
00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:12.648
Nevertheless, public diplomacy cannot compensate indefinitely for policies that foreign audiences perceive as unjust.

70
00:05:13.733 --> 00:05:17.573
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:21.287
This period is often referred blank the 'golden age'.

72
00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:24.683
A, as. B, to as. C, to.

73
00:05:28.233 --> 00:05:30.070
The answer is B: to as.

74
00:05:31.217 --> 00:05:33.994
Shaping a country's international image is blank.

75
00:05:34.294 --> 00:05:38.865
A, nation branding. B, outreach. C, goodwill.

76
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.108
The answer is A: nation branding.

77
00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:49.376
Friendly feelings and support between peoples are blank.

78
00:05:49.676 --> 00:05:54.362
A, perception. B, international relations. C, goodwill.

79
00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.772
The answer is C: goodwill.

80
00:06:00.917 --> 00:06:02.835
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:03.135 --> 00:06:10.596
A, The theory is known as constructivism.. B, The theory is known to as constructivism.. C, The theory knows as constructivism..

82
00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:17.654
The answer is A: The theory is known as constructivism..

83
00:06:18.817 --> 00:06:22.151
According to the reading, who coined the term 'soft power'?

84
00:06:22.451 --> 00:06:26.662
A, Joseph Nye. B, Aristotle. C, Kotter.

85
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.641
The answer is A: Joseph Nye.

86
00:06:33.833 --> 00:06:36.811
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:41.391
What is an example of soft power a country might use in diplomacy?

88
00:06:58.183 --> 00:06:59.927
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:00.227 --> 00:07:39.076
A clear example of soft power is offering university scholarships to foreign students. Japan and Australia, for instance, fund many young Cambodians, who often return home with positive impressions and personal networks. This strategy, sometimes referred to as 'education diplomacy', builds goodwill that can influence trade and political relations for decades. Cultural exports such as films, music and cuisine work similarly. Unlike military or economic pressure, soft power persuades people to want to cooperate.

90
00:07:39.376 --> 00:07:41.201
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:42.117 --> 00:07:47.184
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and sometimes referred to as and known as.

92
00:07:47.484 --> 00:07:53.480
Today's value is diplomacy. Disagree respectfully, and look for solutions everyone can accept.

93
00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.502
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:56.802 --> 00:07:59.129
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

